    <h1>Ravens in the UK: Distribution, Population, and Recovery</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) is recovering strongly in the UK after centuries of persecution. From a low point confined to Wales and remote Scotland, the breeding population has grown to an estimated 7,000–8,000 pairs, with ongoing range expansion into lowland England. Ravens are fully legally protected under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Historical Decline and Persecution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens were once widespread across all of Britain and Ireland, inhabiting urban areas as street scavengers in medieval towns as well as rural and upland habitats. From the 16th century onwards, systematic persecution by gamekeepers, farmers, and poisoning campaigns eliminated ravens from most of England and lowland Scotland by the 19th century. The main causes:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Persecution as livestock predators (particularly at lambing).</li>
      <li>Game preservation — gamekeepers killed ravens as nest-robbers and perceived threats to gamebird eggs and chicks.</li>
      <li>Egg collection — raven eggs were popular collectors' items.</li>
      <li>Poisoned baits set for predators.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>By the early 20th century, UK ravens were largely confined to Wales, parts of northern England, and the Scottish Highlands and islands.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Recovery and Range Expansion</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Legal protection from 1981 (Wildlife and Countryside Act) and the broader decline of intensive gamekeeping and poisoning have driven a substantial recovery:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wales:</strong> always the raven's stronghold in Britain; population remains dense, with ravens abundant on coastal cliffs, upland moorland, and forestry.</li>
      <li><strong>Northern England:</strong> the Lake District, Pennines, North Pennines, and North York Moors have seen population increases and range consolidation since the 1980s.</li>
      <li><strong>Scotland:</strong> population recovered throughout upland Scotland, with ongoing expansion into lowland areas.</li>
      <li><strong>England — recolonisation:</strong> ravens have been breeding in increasing numbers of English counties through the late 20th and early 21st centuries, including areas in the Midlands, East Anglia, and the South West that were raven-free for over a century.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>UK Habitats</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> UK ravens favour:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Upland moorland and mountain:</strong> the classic raven habitat in Wales and Scotland — open ground, rocky outcrops, abundant sheep carrion.</li>
      <li><strong>Sea cliffs:</strong> coastal cliffs around Wales, south-west England, and northern Scotland are important breeding habitat.</li>
      <li><strong>Forestry:</strong> plantation forest edges provide nest trees and roosting sites, with adjacent open moorland for foraging.</li>
      <li><strong>Farmland:</strong> lowland farmland with scattered trees is now used increasingly, particularly by recovering English populations.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Tower of London Ravens</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The most famous ravens in the UK are the resident ravens of the Tower of London — currently maintained under the care of the Ravenmaster. The legend that "if the ravens of the Tower should be lost, the Crown will fall" is a tradition documented from the 1880s, not from medieval sources. See <a href="/lore/world/tower-of-london-ravens/">Tower of London Ravens</a> for the full history.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
